        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains under acute operational stress. The Crisis Pressure index sits in the extreme band, flat over the past 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as its top driver. The Escalation Forecast index, a horizon-mix composite, reads in the elevated band and is rising over 24 hours, though the signal carries a low-confidence flag due to thin contract volume, with Kalshi forward contracts as the top contributor. The two indices diverge: current pressure is extreme while the forward-looking composite is elevated, a gap worth watching. Port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides totaled 697 vessels in the most recent 24 hours. IMF PortWatch recorded 2 transits on 2026-08-02, against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; PortWatch publishes weekly on Tuesdays, so a multi-day lag is expected. At 14:52 UTC, 208 vessels were holding position away from berth, a figure that excludes ships within 25 km of a working port. Brent fell 1.77% over 24 hours to $78.84 as of 14:52 UTC. On the diplomatic side, unverified single-source reports citing NBC affiliates and The Globe and Mail quote President Trump saying a deal could come as early as Wednesday; no parties to any prospective agreement are named in those headlines, so the counterparty remains unspecified. Separately, a gCaptain report describes a growing flotilla of Iranian oil tankers gathering off Iran's coast, consistent with US naval blockade pressure on Tehran's exports. A Houthi claim of an attack on a Saudi tanker in the Red Sea, carried by pakistantoday.com.pk and tribune.com.pk, is unverified and not confirmed by independent or US sources.",
